; M1e S1e.2.1 — `(absent …)` over two premises.
;
; `(absent P)` is negation as failure over **one** premise; everything past
; the first argument was dropped. This rule reads as "no q and no p", and
; `(p A)` is stated below, so a reader expects it not to fire. Before S1e.2.1
; it fired — the second conjunct was never compiled, never matched and never
; mentioned.
;
; The fix for a program that means the conjunction is to write it: wrap the
; two in an `(and …)`, which is what `std.macro`'s `forall` expands to.
(relation p T)
(relation q T)

(rule two-subject-naf ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent (q ?x) (p ?x)))
  :assert (q ?x))

(p A)
